113	 *
114	 * Asks by head branch instead of scanning the repository's pull requests: that
115	 * list is paginated newest-first, so a branch whose pull request has since
116	 * fallen off the first page would read as unmerged. The number of branches one
117	 * issue owns is small, and this is only reached for those.
118	 *
119	 * ## Why the tip is part of the question
120	 *
121	 * "A pull request from this branch merged" and "this branch's work is in the base"
122	 * are different facts, and the name makes them look like one. A branch name is
123	 * reused: a parent issue's branch exists for as long as its children keep merging
124	 * into it, and a merge to the base can land part of it while the branch goes on
125	 * receiving work. Asking the first question answered *merged* for a tip carrying
126	 * undelivered work, so the parent's aggregation run found nothing to resume and
127	 * started on the base branch — where `create_pr` then had a head equal to its base
128	 * and could not open the pull request that delivers the branch (#441, #476).
129	 *
130	 * So the branch is merged when the commit it points at NOW is the commit a pull
131	 * request landed. A merged pull request from this branch whose `head.sha` is some
132	 * earlier commit says only that the branch has moved on since.
133	 *
134	 * A failed read stays "unmerged": resuming a branch that turned out to have
135	 * nothing new costs a run that starts one branch too early, and reporting a branch
